---
name: view
description: >-
  Build a JRebirth View (scene graph, controls, bindings, view-local CSS).
  Use when composing nodes for a feature, or attaching a stylesheet to a view.
---
# Create View

The View builds the scene graph and composes the visual structure of a feature. It is a pure rendering role: it constructs nodes, binds controls to Model properties, and exposes action controls for the Controller to wire.

## What belongs in a View

- Panes, controls, labels, tables, forms, and other JavaFX nodes.
- Visual structure and layout composition.
- Simple binding hookup to Model properties (e.g., `label.textProperty().bind(model().nameProperty())`).
- Styling hooks such as CSS class assignments.

## What does not belong in a View

- Business rules or validation logic (belongs in the Model).
- Persistent or complex state (belongs in the Model).
- Persistence and backend calls (belong in a Service).
- Heavy imperative workflow or multi-step orchestration (belongs in a Command).

## Key API

- Extend `DefaultView<M, Pane, C>`. Build all nodes in `initView()`. Expose controls via package-private accessors so the Controller can wire them.
- Annotate action controls with `@OnAction` so the Controller receives those events automatically.
- Attach a view-local stylesheet with `addCSS(StyleSheetItem)` from inside the View. This adds the stylesheet to `pane().getStylesheets()`.
- Build the `StyleSheetItem` with `Resources.create(new StyleSheet("basename"))`. Add `.module(...)` when the resource is in a different module. Use the one-argument `addCSS(StyleSheetItem)` form here; the two-argument `addCSS(scene, ...)` form is for the Application only.

Template: `templates/ViewTemplate.java.txt`.
